       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 4,250 mg three times daily (12,750 mg/day) is five times the maximum labeled dose. Metformin doesn't work better above about 2,550 mg/day, and doses in this range carry a serious risk of lactic acidosis. I can't see a clinical reason that would make this safe for a 70 kg patient, and I'd be uncomfortable putting it into the system as a routine order.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range now.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals, or a lower dose if you'd like to titrate.\n- **Add a note** to any order I enter, such as \"titrate per glycemic response\" or \"dose reviewed with pharmacy.\"\n- **Route the original dose through pharmacy.** If you do have a reason for the higher dose, a pharmacist or your hospital's override process should review it directly, not have me enter it.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
